{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd && git status --short && rg -n -i \\\"P50|normal faithful corner trace|292ec9c3|d5eadb4d\\\" <original-home>/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md || true && sed -n '1,240p' <original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,220p' <original-home>/.codex/skills/proof-audit-compile-correctness/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"lean/stabilizer\n M aristotle_tasks/P49-finite-corner-trace.md\n M aristotle_tasks/P50-normal-faithful-corner-trace.md\n?? AristotleCandidates/FiniteCornerTrace.lean\n?? AristotleCandidates/KaplanskyDensity.lean\n?? AristotleCandidates/LocalHyperbolicAlgebraicAlpha.lean\n?? AristotleCandidates/MixedUHFHyperfiniteFactor.lean\n?? AristotleCandidates/StrongOperatorClosedVonNeumann.lean\n?? AristotleCandidates/VonNeumannBicommutantTheorem.lean\n?? reviews/P49-finite-corner-trace-closeout.md\n?? reviews/P49-finite-corner-trace-recovery.md\n?? reviews/P49A-strong-operator-closed-von-neumann-closeout.md\n?? reviews/P49B-von-neumann-bicommutant-theorem-closeout.md\n?? reviews/P49C-kaplansky-density-closeout.md\n?? reviews/P50-normal-faithful-corner-trace-closeout.md\n?? reviews/P53-local-hyperbolic-algebraic-alpha-closeout.md\n?? reviews/P58-mixed-uhf-hyperfinite-factor-closeout.md\n---\nname: aristotle-cli\ndescription: Use when Codex should interact with the Aristotle CLI to submit Lean projects or papers, request sorry filling or formalization runs, inspect job status, and download results for iterative project work.\n---\n\n# Aristotle CLI\n\nUse Aristotle for remote Lean/project runs when the local repo is ready but needs automated proof search, sorry filling, or formalization help.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the target project or paper from the current workspace.\n2. Check the local build or project state first. Prefer a narrow local `lake build` or equivalent before submitting anything remote.\n3. Use the bundled wrapper `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh` unless `ARISTOTLE_API_KEY` is already known to be exported in the current process.\n4. Submit the smallest useful task prompt. Keep it concrete and tied to the files at hand.\n5. Wait for the result when that is useful. Otherwise poll or list jobs.\n6. Download artifacts, inspect the diffs, and rerun local verification.\n\n## Commands\n\nUse the commands in [references/aristotle-cli.md](references/aristotle-cli.md).\n\n## Operating rules\n\n- Prefer `aristotle submit` for Lean project work, especially sorry filling or proof search.\n- Prefer `aristotle formalize` when the input is a paper or TeX source that should become a Lean artifact.\n- Use `aristotle list` to inspect recent jobs and `aristotle download` to retrieve results.\n- Keep prompts specific: name the file, theorem, section, or proof obligation instead of asking for broad improvement.\n- If a run fails, inspect the local artifact and re-submit only after the prompt or project state is corrected.\n- Do not print or log the API key.\n- If authentication fails but `~/.bashrc` contains the key, do not assume sourcing `.bashrc` works. Many `.bashrc` files return early for noninteractive shells. Use `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh`.\n\n## When to escalate back to the user\n\n- The CLI is missing, or authentication still fails after trying `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh`.\n- The target repo is not a Lean project or paper and needs clarification.\n- The requested action would overwrite user work outside the target project tree.\n---\nname: proof-audit-compile-correctness\ndescription: Audit mathematical proof files and derivations for logical correctness while separating TeX/Lean/build success from truth. Use when the user asks to check a named proof file, verify whether a proof is correct, red-team a theorem note, inspect circularity, compare compile success to mathematical validity, or prepare closed-book proof-agent prompts.\n---\n\n# Proof Audit Compile Correctness\n\nAudit the exact artifact named by the user. Do not treat a compiled PDF as evidence that the proof is correct.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Read the named proof file and nearby status/context files.\n2. Compile or build if relevant, and record build status separately.\n3. Identify the theorem statement, assumptions, definitions, and claimed route.\n4. Check each load-bearing step for:\n   - hidden assumptions;\n   - circular use of the target theorem;\n   - unjustified reductions;\n   - domain/codomain mismatches;\n   - convention changes;\n   - numerical evidence promoted to proof;\n   - stale status claims contradicted by later audits.\n5. If a proof fails, identify the first fatal gap, not just a list of concerns.\n6. If useful, create or suggest a minimal next artifact: lemma statement, counterexample search, standalone TeX extraction, Lean target, or closed-book proof prompt.\n\n## Status Labels\n\nUse direct labels:\n\n- `compiled_not_checked`;\n- `compiled_but_incorrect`;\n- `proof_gap_found`;\n- `numerical_evidence_only`;\n- `candidate_proof_needs_review`;\n- `approved_with_residual_risks`;\n- `proved` only when the argument survives the audit.\n\n## Closed-Book Proof Prompts\n\nWhen preparing prompts for outside proof agents, do not mention that a problem is open unless the user asks. Emphasize:\n\n- no internet/literature lookup;\n- solve from the statement;\n- avoid circularity and stronger false lemmas;\n- report failed approaches explicitly;\n- provide exact proof or exact counterexample.\n\n## Final Report\n\nLead with findings ordered by severity. Include file/location references, build status, correctness verdict, and the next concrete proof task.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
