ctional on it.\nFor that, let $x \\in M_\\G$. By Kaplanskys Density theorem\n\\cite[I.9.1.3]{Blackadar}, there exist a uniformly bounded net $(x_i)_{i\\in I}$ in $\\cU_\\G$ with $x_i \\xrightarrow{\\sigma\\text{-}\\mathrm{s}} x$ and hence\nstrongly. By the same argument as in the previous lemma then\n\\begin{equation}\n    q x_i q \\xrightarrow{\\sigma\\text{-}\\mathrm{s}} q x q,\n\\end{equation}\nstrongly which proves that $q \\cU_\\G q$ is strongly dense in $q M_\\G q$. The\nsame argument implies that for every $a,b \\in M_\\G$ there exist uniformly bounded nets $(a_i)_{i\\in I},(b_j)_{j\\in J}$ in $\\cU_\\G$ such that\n\\begin{equation}\n   q a_i q \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{s}} q a q,\n   \\qquad\n   q b_j q \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{s}} q b q.\n\\end{equation}\nTo argue for the convergence of the product, we can introduce a joint index $k$\\footnote{For example $k\n    = (i,j)$ so that $a_{(i,j)} = a_i, b_{(i,j)} = b_j$ and $(i',j') \\preceq (i,j)\n    \\Leftrightarrow i' \\preceq i \\land j' \\preceq j$},\nso that by the same argument as in the proof of Lemma \\ref{lem:finite-trace}\n\\begin{equation}\n    q a_i q b_j q \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{s}} q a q b q,\n    \\qquad\n    q b_j q a_i q \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{s}} q b q a q.\n\\end{equation}s\nSince the nets $q a_n q, q\nb_n q$ are uniformly bounded and the product on uniformly bounded sets is\nstrongly continuous \\LS{REF},\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\t_q( (q a q ) (q b q)) &= \\bra{\\W}  (q a q ) (q b q)\\ket{\\W} \\\\\n                                & = \\lim_{k \\rightarrow \\infty}\\bra{\\W}  (q a_k\n                                q ) (q b_k q)\\ket{\\W} \\\\\n                                & = \\lim_{k \\rightarrow \\infty}\\bra{\\W}  (q b_k\n                                q ) (q a_k q)\\ket{\\W} \\\\\n                                &= \\bra{\\W}  (q b q ) (q a q)\\ket{\\W}\n    \\end{aligned}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere we used strong convergence of the nets in the second line which proves\ncyclicity on $q M_\\G q$. Since $\\t_q$ is a restriction of a vector functional\nto $qM_\\G q$ it is normal. Since on $q\\cH_\\w, q$ is the identity, we have that $\\t(1) =\n\\bra{\\W} q \\ket{\\W} = \\langle \\W | \\W \\rangle = 1$ by Lemma \\ref{lem:q_exists}.\nThis implies that $\\t_q$ defines a normal state on the von Neumann algebra $qM_\\G q$. \n\\end{proof}\n\n\\subsection{Faithfulness and finiteness}\nNow that we established that the state $\\t_q$ is tracial for $q M_\\G q$, we\nwant to use it to restrict the full algebra $M_\\G$. The central decomposition\nof $M_\\G$ asserts that we can decompose our Hilbert space into an integral\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\cH_\\w \\simeq \\int_X \\cH_x d\\m(x),\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $X$ is a measurable space equipped with a measure $\\m$, such that for\neach measurable set $E \\subset X$ one obtains a projection $z_E$ which is in\nthe center of $M_\\G$ such that\n\\begin{equation}\n    z_E \\cH_\\w \\simeq \\int_E \\cH_x d\\m(x).\n\\end{equation}\nSimilarly one has a decomposition \n\\begin{equation}\n    M_\\G \\simeq \\int_X M_x d\\m(x),\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $M_x \\subset \\cB(\\cH_x)$ is a factor almost surely. Our goal is to proof\nthat none of the $M_x$ can have type III. To do so, we will proof that every\none of the subalgebras appearing in the central decomposition has a finite\nprojection, which arises from our $q$, which cannot happen for type III\nalgebras \\footnote{They are defined as not having finite projections}.\nThe finiteness of a projection means that if there exist a partial isometry $v$\nwith\n\\begin{equation}\n    v^\\dagger v = q, \\quad v v^\\dagger \\leq q,\n\\end{equation}\nthen $v v^\\dagger = q$. A simple way to see finiteness is the existence of a\nfaithful tracial state $\\t$, since then\n\\begin{equation}\n    \\t(q) = \\t(v v^\\dagger) = \\t(v^\\dagger v) \\Rightarrow \\t(q-v^\\dagger v) =\n    0.\n\\end{equation}\nIf $\\t$ is faithful, i.e. for $x \\geq 0$, $\\t(x) = 0$ iff $x = 0$, this implies $q = v^\\dagger v$.\nWe therefore need \n\\begin{proposition}\\label{prop:faithful}\n$\\tau_q$ is faithful on $q\\cM_\\Gamma q$.\n\\end{proposition}\n\\begin{proof}\nLet $x\\in q\\cM_\\Gamma q$ satisfy\n\\begin{equation}\n  \\tau_q(x^\\dagger x)=0.\n  \\label{eq:faithfulness-zero}\n\\end{equation}\nWe want to show that $x = 0$. We will do so, by demonstrating that $x \\ket{\\x}\n= 0$ for a dense set of state $\\ket{\\x}$. This dense set \nwill be made of states of the form\n\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:dense_states}\n    \\ket{\\x_{a,b}} = \\pi_\\w(a) \\pi_\\w(b) \\ket{\\W},\n\\end{equation}\n with $a \\in \\cA_\\G, b \\in \\cA_{\\G^c}$, which form a norm-dense set of $\\cA$\n and since we consider the GNS Hilbert space of $\\cA$ with respect to $\\w$, these states form a\n dense set in $\\cH_\\w$.\nFor $y\\in q\\cM_\\Gamma q$ we have \n\\begin{align}\\label{eq:xyOmega_zero}\n    \\norm{xy\\Omega}^2\n  &=\\tau_q(y^\\dagger x^\\dagger xy)\\\\\n  &=\\tau_q(x^\\dagger xy y^\\dagger)\\\\\n  &=\\tau_q\\big((x^\\dagger x)^{1/2}yy^\\dagger(x^\\dagger x)^{1/2}\\big)\\\\\n  &\\le \\|y\\|^2\\tau_q(x^*x)=0.\n\\end{align}\nIn the second line we used the definition of the state $\\t_q$, in the third we\nused traciality of $\\t$ on $q M_\\G q$. In the fourth line we used that every\npositive operator has a square root together with traciality. In the fourth\nline we used that \n\\begin{equation}\n    \\norm{y}^2 \\id \\geq y^\\dagger y,\n\\end{equation}\nwhich follows from the definition of the operator norm. Conjugating the\npositive operator $ O_y = \\norm{y}^2 \\id - y^\\dagger y$ by $(x^\\dagger x)^{1/2}$ again leads to a\npositive operator. Since $\\t_q$ is the restriction of a vector functional, it\nis positive, which applied to the positivity of\n$x^\\dagger O_y x$ implies the last line.\nWe thus have found that for every $y\\in q\\cM_\\Gamma q$\n\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:all_xyOmega_zero}\n  xy\\ket{\\W}=0.\n\\end{equation}\nNow for general $a\\in\\cA_\\Gamma$, we note that $x=xq$, since $x \\in q M_\\G q$\nand $q\\ket{\\W}=\\ket{\\W}$ by Lemma \\ref{lem:q_exists}. This implies that\n\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:x_a_zero}\n  x\\pi_\\w(a)\\ket{\\W}\n  =x q\\pi_\\w(a) q \\ket{\\W}=0.\n\\end{equation}\nSince operators in $\\cA_\\G$ commute with operators in $\\cA_{\\G^c}$ and $\\pi_\\w$\nis a representation of $\\cA$, we have that\n\\begin{equation}\n  \\cM_\\Gamma\n  =\\pi_\\omega(\\cA_\\Gamma)''\n  \\subseteq\n  \\pi_\\omega(\\cA_{\\Gamma^c})'.\n  \\label{eq:M-commutes-complement}\n\\end{equation}\nSince $x \\in q M_\\G q \\subset M_\\G$, it commutes with\n$\\pi_\\omega(\\cA_{\\Gamma^c})$.  For $a\\in\\cA_\\Gamma$ and $b\\in\\cA_{\\Gamma^c}$ we\ntherefore have on the dense set of states from Eq. \\eqref{eq:dense_states}\n\\begin{equation} \\label{eq:x_global_annihilation}\n  x\\pi_\\omega(a)\\pi_\\omega(b)\\ket{\\W}\n  =\\pi_\\omega(b)x\\pi_\\omega(a)\\ket{\\W} = 0,\n\\end{equation}\nwhich implies $x = 0$ and thus proves faithfulness.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
